{"id": "swebq0056", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.14, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2478, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Salt Lake Golden Eagles;Salt Lake City Gulls;Utah Utes men's basketball;Salt Lake City Angels;Utah Grizzlies;Real Salt Lake;Utah Jazz", "query": "what there to do in salt lake city utah?", "transcript": "What there to do in Salt Lake City, Utah?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Salt Lake City has a great mix of outdoor, cultural, historical, and food activities. Popular things to do include visiting Temple Square, exploring the Utah State Capitol for city views, checking out the Natural History Museum of Utah with its dinosaur exhibits, strolling through Red Butte Garden, or hiking nearby peaks like Ensign Peak. In winter, you can ski at resorts like Alta or Snowbird, and in summer there's Antelope Island State Park on the Great Salt Lake. Downtown offers plenty of restaurants, breweries, and events too. It's really easy to get from the city center to nature within just a short drive.", "expert_answer": "Salt Lake City, Utah has a great mix of outdoor, cultural, historical, and food activities. Popular things to do include:\n\n- **Visit Temple Square** – Historic center of the city with gardens, architecture, and visitor centers.\n- **Explore the Utah State Capitol** – Great views of the city and mountains.\n- **See the Natural History Museum of Utah** – Excellent dinosaur, geology, and Native culture exhibits.\n- **Walk around Red Butte Garden** – Botanical gardens near the University of Utah.\n- **Go hiking nearby** – Popular spots include **Ensign Peak**, **Big Cottonwood Canyon**, and **Little Cottonwood Canyon**.\n- **Visit the Great Salt Lake** – Check out **Antelope Island State Park** for lake views, wildlife, and bison.\n- **Ski or snowboard in winter** – Nearby resorts include **Alta**, **Snowbird**, **Brighton**, and **Solitude**.\n- **Check out downtown food and drinks** – Try local restaurants, breweries, coffee shops, and the downtown farmers market if in season.\n- **Tour the Leonardo Museum or Clark Planetarium** – Good options for families or a rainy day.\n- **Catch a game or event** – Utah Jazz basketball, Real Salt Lake soccer, concerts, and shows.\n\nIf you like nature, Salt Lake City is especially great because you can be downtown in the morning and hiking or skiing in the mountains within 30–45 minutes.", "expert_latency_s": 8.86, "stall_ms": 23, "relay_ms": 3080}
{"id": "swebq0059", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.04, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.3594, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Ronnie Turner;Craig Hill", "query": "where are ike and tina turner's children?", "transcript": "Where are Ike and Tina Turner's children?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Ike and Tina Turner had a blended family of four sons. Two of their children, Craig and Ronnie, passed away in recent years. The other two, Ike Jr. and Michael, are still alive but tend to keep a low profile these days.", "expert_answer": "Ike and Tina Turner had a blended family of four sons:\n\n- **Craig Raymond Turner** — Tina’s son, adopted by Ike; **died in 2018**.\n- **Ronnie Turner** — Ike and Tina’s only biological child together; **died in 2022**.\n- **Ike Turner Jr.** — Ike’s son, adopted/raised by Tina; a musician/producer, reportedly living a private life in the U.S.\n- **Michael Turner** — Ike’s son, adopted/raised by Tina; lives largely out of the public eye.\n\nSo, **two of the children, Craig and Ronnie, have died; Ike Jr. and Michael are still living but mostly private.**", "expert_latency_s": 13.06, "stall_ms": 23, "relay_ms": 1183}
{"id": "swebq0062", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.32, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1274, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Lojban;Esperanto Language;English Language", "query": "what language do australia people speak?", "transcript": "What language do Australia people speak?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Most people in Australia speak English. Australia has no official language at the federal level, but English is the main and de facto national language. Many other languages are also spoken, including Indigenous Australian languages and languages brought by immigrant communities.", "expert_answer": "Most people in Australia speak **English**. Australia has no official language at the federal level, but **English is the main and de facto national language**. Many other languages are also spoken, including Indigenous Australian languages and languages brought by immigrant communities.", "expert_latency_s": 1.32, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 1059}
