{"id": "swebq0068", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 3.3, "n_chunks": 4, "n_concurrent": 4, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1693, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Mathematics;Algebra", "query": "what area of science did sir isaac newton study?", "transcript": "What area of science did Sir Isaac Newton study?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Sir Isaac Newton was a key figure in the study of physics and mathematics.", "expert_answer": "Sir Isaac Newton studied **physics and mathematics**, especially **mechanics, gravity, and motion**.", "expert_latency_s": 1.0, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 363}
{"id": "swebq0071", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.83, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2112, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "This suggests that by 1438 Gutenberg and Dritzehen had in all probability already invented a printing process using moveable type (separate pieces of wood or metal for each letter of the alphabet).", "query": "when was the printing press invented by gutenberg?", "transcript": "When was the printing press invented by Guttenberg?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The printing press was invented by Johannes Gutenberg around 1440 in Mainz, Germany.", "expert_answer": "Johannes Gutenberg invented the printing press around **1440** in Mainz, Germany.", "expert_latency_s": 1.81, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 471}
{"id": "swebq0074", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.46, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1954, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Central European Time;UTC+01:00", "query": "what timezone is sweden gmt?", "transcript": "What time zone is Sweden GMT?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Sweden is in the Central European Time zone. In winter it's one hour ahead of GMT, and during summer they observe daylight saving time, so it becomes two hours ahead.", "expert_answer": "Sweden is in the **Central European Time (CET)** zone:\n\n- **GMT+1** during standard time (winter)\n- **GMT+2** during daylight saving time (summer), called **CEST**", "expert_latency_s": 1.95, "stall_ms": 23, "relay_ms": 811}
{"id": "swebq0077", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 4.22, "n_chunks": 5, "n_concurrent": 5, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2233, "eot_read_ms": 21, "reference_answer": "Presidential system;Federal republic;Representative democracy;Two-party system;Constitutional republic;Republic", "query": "what type of government does the us constitution set up?", "transcript": "What type of government does the US Constitution set up?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "The U.S. Constitution sets up a federal democratic republic. It's structured as a representative democracy with separation of powers among the three branches and uses federalism to divide power between the national and state governments.", "expert_answer": "The U.S. Constitution sets up a **federal democratic republic**—specifically, a system with **representative democracy**, **separation of powers** among three branches, and **federalism** dividing power between the national and state governments.", "expert_latency_s": 1.53, "stall_ms": 23, "relay_ms": 953}
{"id": "swebq0080", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 2.41, "n_chunks": 3, "n_concurrent": 3, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1814, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "20003", "query": "what are republicans views on health care?", "transcript": "What are Republicans' views on health care?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Republicans have a range of views on health care, but many tend to favor a system with less government involvement and more market-based solutions. They often support private insurance options, price transparency, and consumer choice, while being skeptical of large government-run programs like single-payer systems. A lot of Republicans also want to limit regulations they see as burdensome, though some still back key protections for people with preexisting conditions.", "expert_answer": "Republicans’ views on health care vary, but common themes include:\n\n- **Less government involvement:** Many Republicans prefer limiting the federal government’s role in health care.\n- **Market-based reforms:** They often support competition among private insurers, price transparency, and consumer choice.\n- **Opposition to “Medicare for All”:** Most Republicans oppose single-payer government-run health care systems.\n- **Repealing or changing the Affordable Care Act (ACA):** Republicans have often criticized the ACA, especially mandates and regulations, though views differ on what should replace it.\n- **Lower costs through deregulation:** They commonly argue that reducing regulations can lower premiums and expand options.\n- **Health savings accounts (HSAs):** Many support expanding HSAs so individuals can save tax-free for medical expenses.\n- **State flexibility:** Republicans often favor giving states more control over Medicaid and health policy.\n- **Protecting certain popular benefits:** Many Republican politicians say they support protections for people with preexisting conditions, though they may differ from Democrats on how to achieve that.\n\nIn short, Republicans generally favor a more private-sector, consumer-choice approach to health care, while Democrats more often support expanded government coverage and regulation.", "expert_latency_s": 8.23, "stall_ms": 23, "relay_ms": 1828}
