{"id": "swebq0203", "pool": "swebq", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 1.67, "n_chunks": 2, "n_concurrent": 2, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.1198, "eot_read_ms": 20, "reference_answer": "Philips Arena;Georgia Dome;Omni Coliseum;Zoo Atlanta;Grant Park;Castleberry Hill;Atlanta-Fulton County Stadium;Little Five Points;Turner Field;Buckhead", "query": "what to visit in atlanta?", "transcript": "What to visit in Atlanta?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "Atlanta has so many great spots to check out! If you're into aquariums, the Georgia Aquarium is a must—huge and full of amazing marine life. For pop culture history, World of Coca-Cola is fun with interactive exhibits and tastings from all over. The Martin Luther King Jr. National Historical Park is incredibly moving, showing his legacy in detail. Nature lovers will enjoy Piedmont Park or the Atlanta Botanical Garden nearby. The BeltLine Eastside Trail is awesome for walking or biking, lined with murals and cool restaurants. Ponce City Market offers food, shopping, and great views, while Centennial Olympic Park sits right downtown. History buffs can explore the Atlanta History Center or take in some art at the High Museum. And if you're into live entertainment, don't miss the Fox Theatre or Fernbank Museum's natural history exhibits. There's something for everyone!", "expert_answer": "Here are some top places to visit in Atlanta:\n\n- **Georgia Aquarium** – One of the largest aquariums in the world, with whale sharks, belugas, and immersive exhibits.\n- **World of Coca-Cola** – Interactive museum about Coca-Cola’s history, including tastings from around the world.\n- **Martin Luther King Jr. National Historical Park** – Includes Dr. King’s birth home, Ebenezer Baptist Church, and exhibits on the Civil Rights Movement.\n- **Atlanta Botanical Garden** – Beautiful gardens, seasonal displays, and the Canopy Walk near Piedmont Park.\n- **Piedmont Park** – Atlanta’s central green space, great for walking, skyline views, and picnics.\n- **High Museum of Art** – Major art museum with strong collections and rotating exhibitions.\n- **BeltLine Eastside Trail** – Popular walking/biking trail with murals, restaurants, breweries, and shops.\n- **Ponce City Market** – Food hall, shopping, and rooftop amusement-style attractions with great city views.\n- **Centennial Olympic Park** – Downtown park from the 1996 Olympics, near several major attractions.\n- **Fox Theatre** – Historic theater with beautiful architecture; great for concerts, Broadway shows, and tours.\n- **Atlanta History Center** – Excellent museum complex in Buckhead, including historic homes and gardens.\n- **Fernbank Museum of Natural History** – Dinosaurs, science exhibits, and an outdoor forest experience.\n\nIf you like neighborhoods, check out **Little Five Points** for eclectic shops and nightlife, **Inman Park** for restaurants and historic homes, and **Old Fourth Ward** for the BeltLine, food, and nightlife.", "expert_latency_s": 10.12, "stall_ms": 22, "relay_ms": 4487}
